The rules for testing suspending functions are quite simple. 
Suppose a function transforms a repository Flow:
fun observeAppointments(): Flow<List<Appointment>> =
    appointmentRepository
        .observeAppointments()
        .filterIsInstance<AppointmentsUpdate>()
        .map { it.appointments }
        .distinctUntilChanged()
        .retry {
            it is ApiException && it.code in 500..599
        }
The tests should check each important behavior:
1>keep only appointment updates
2>remove repeated values
3>retry for 5XX API errors
Using flowOf() as test data:
val repo = FakeAppointmentRepository(
    flowOf(
        AppointmentsConfirmed,
        AppointmentsUpdate(listOf(anAppointment1)),
        AppointmentsUpdate(listOf(anAppointment2)),
        AppointmentsConfirmed
    )
)
Then collect the result:
val result = service.observeAppointments().toList()
And compare it:
assertEquals(expected, result)
flowOf() is useful because it creates a finite Flow where time is not important.
runTest(): For Flow tests involving coroutines or delays:
@Test
fun testSomething() = runTest {
    // test code
}
runTest provides the test coroutine environment and is especially important when testing time-dependent Flow behavior.
A simple toList() test may not detect an unwanted delay.
Example:
.onEach { delay(1000) }
A normal list-style test can still pass even though the Flow became slower. To test timing:
val result = service.observeAppointments()
    .map { currentTime to it }
    .toList()
Then verify expected times:
assertEquals(
    listOf(
        1000L to listOf(anAppointment1),
        2000L to listOf(anAppointment2),
        3000L to listOf(anAppointment1)
    ),
    result
)
This checks when values are emitted, not only which values are emitted.
Retrying can produce an infinite Flow, so directly using toList() can never finish. Use take() to limit the number of emitted elements:
val result = service.observeAppointments()
    .take(3)
    .toList()
Example:
flow {
    emit(AppointmentsUpdate(listOf(anAppointment1)))
    throw ApiException(502, "Some message")
}
Because the exception is retryable, the first appointment can appear repeatedly.We should also test that non-API exceptions are not retried and API exceptions with non-5XX codes are not retried. We can aslo use catch to collect the final exception as part of the test result.
val result = service.observeAppointments()
    .catch<Any> { emit(it) }
    .toList()
StateFlow and SharedFlow are often infinite. They do not normally complete, so .toList() can wait forever. When using runTest, the book recommends using backgroundScope for these long-running flows.
This can hang:
val result = service.observeMessages("0")
    .toList()
because the Flow never completes.
It can hide ordering or unexpected extra values because it only checks the requested number of elements.
Another approach is to store emitted values:
val emittedMessages = mutableListOf<Message>()
service.observeMessages("0")
    .onEach { emittedMessages.add(it) }
    .launchIn(backgroundScope)
Then test at different times:
delay(1)
assertEquals(
    listOf(Message("0", "A")),
    emittedMessages
)
Later:
delay(1000)
assertEquals(
    listOf(
        Message("0", "A"),
        Message("0", "C")
    ),
    emittedMessages
)
This gives more control over what was emitted and when.
Create a helper function for collecting a Flow only for a certain duration:
suspend fun <T> Flow<T>.toListDuring(
    duration: Duration
): List<T> = coroutineScope {
    val result = mutableListOf<T>()
    val job = launch {
        this@toListDuring.collect(result::add)
    }
    delay(duration)
    job.cancel()
    return@coroutineScope result
}
Usage:
val emittedMessages =
    service.observeMessages("0")
        .toListDuring(1.milliseconds)
This is a simpler alternative when you want to observe a Flow for a specific amount of time.
Turbine, a library designed to simplify Flow testing, can aslo be used.
Example:
val messagesTurbine =
    service.observeMessages("0")
        .testIn(backgroundScope)
assertEquals(
    Message("0", "A"),
    messagesTurbine.awaitItem()
)
assertEquals(
    Message("0", "C"),
    messagesTurbine.awaitItem()
)
messagesTurbine.expectNoEvents()
Suppose shareIn() should ensure that only one connection to the source exists.
private val source = messagesSource
    .shareIn(
        scope = scope,
        started = SharingStarted.WhileSubscribed()
    )
You can count active connections with:
.onStart { connectionsCounter++ }
.onCompletion { connectionsCounter-- }
Then start multiple observers:
service.observeMessages("0").launchIn(backgroundScope)
service.observeMessages("1").launchIn(backgroundScope)
service.observeMessages("0").launchIn(backgroundScope)
service.observeMessages("2").launchIn(backgroundScope)
Finally:
assertEquals(1, connectionsCounter)
This verifies that only one source connection is active.
Another useful approach for ViewModels is to use a MutableSharedFlow as the test source.
val source = MutabeSharedFlow<Message>()
Pass it into a fake service:
val viewModel = ChatViewModel(
    messagesService = FakeMessagesService(source)
)
Start the ViewModel:
viewModel.start("0")
Initially:
assertEquals(null, viewModel.lastMessage.value)
assertEquals(emptyList(), viewModel.messages.value)
Then emit test data:
source.emit(
    Message(fromUserId = "0", text = "ABC")
)
Check the ViewModel:
assertEquals("ABC", viewModel.lastMessage.value)
assertEquals(listOf("ABC"), viewModel.messages.value)
This allows the test to directly control which messages enter the ViewModel.